  "method": {
    "workload": "Each request rereads one committed 9,000,000-row upload. PostgreSQL restarted once after seed/checkpoint. Same volume and image throughout; OS cache not cleared; fresh API container and one client process for each group.",
    "sdk": "Both variants run the same benchmark with the same Python and HTTP library versions. The SDK streams one row at a time; the script checks exact consecutive row numbers from 1 through 9,000,000 and hashes/counts the response bytes at the HTTP transport. All twenty-two responses matched the reference. The bodies were not stored.",
    "timing": "Readiness and receipt checks outside the timer; barrier coordinates starts of parallel requests; the group timer ends after the last response. Main order before 1/2/4 then after 4/2/1. Four-stream groups were repeated in reverse order (after, before); each four-stream summary metric is a median of the corresponding two runs. One run per variant at 1 and 2. Historical throughput is for reference only.",
    "cpu": "Seconds from the change in each Docker cgroup usage_usec or Python process_time for the client, divided by group wall time for average cores. Docker counters bracket the client invocation and include measurement overhead and incidental database activity.",
    "memory": "API PID 1 VmHWM, client process ru_maxrss, and samples about every 0.5 s of PostgreSQL container memory.current (includes page cache charged to the container cgroup) and sum of RssAnon for application-role PostgreSQL backends (avoids summing shared RSS). Active backend samples are process titles other than idle, including idle in transaction between cursor fetches.",
    "postgres_io": "pg_stat_database deltas include diagnostics and background activity. Buffer misses may be served from the OS page cache."
  },
